    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d148">

                <pb facs="0407" n="379" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v11/pg379"/>

                <head>148. Memorandum From President Kennedy to Secretary of Defense McNamara<anchor
                        n="0" xml:id="d148-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1962-11-05">November 5,
                        1962</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>We must operate on the presumption that the Russians may try again. This time
                    they may prepare themselves for action on the sea in the <pb facs="0408" n="380"
                        xml:id="pg380"/> Cuban area. Does Admiral Anderson think they could build up
                    a secret subterranean base which will put them on a near parity with us if we
                    should once again blockade. If he thinks there is substantial danger of this
                    what suggestions would he now make?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">John F. Kennedy</hi><anchor n="1"
                            xml:id="d148-f2"/></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d148-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d148-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Kennedy Library, National Security Files, Cuba, General, 11/1/62-11/6/62.
                    Top Secret.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d148-f2" n="1" xml:id="d148-f2-content">
                <p>Printed from a copy that indicates President Kennedy signed the original. Another
                    note in the same hand reads: “Despatched by receipt #1073—11/15/63—3:30
                    p.m.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
